-- chem/identity.cub — chemical identity as a construction (docs/chemical-identity.md,
-- chemistry-knowledge-graph). The defect is the field's own, stated in its literature: a molecule is
-- carried as a digest of a labelled graph, the labelling is then discarded, and with it the
-- automorphisms that decide which observations of an atom are well defined (Ivanciuc, reported in
-- Krotko 2020: only explicit automorphism determination gets every symmetry class right; O'Boyle
-- 2012: no standard canonical SMILES, each toolkit its own; the InChIKey's published collision
-- rate). Here identity is constructed, not a string compared:
--   Mol            a molecule as a finite structured graph that keeps its automorphisms: elements
--                  and bonds over a type of atoms with a cardinality, not a chosen numbering.
--   Auto           an automorphism: a relabelling with its inverse that preserves element and bond.
--                  auto-id, auto-comp, auto-inv give its group structure.
--   SameOrbit      two atoms related by some automorphism, an equivalence (same-orbit-refl, -sym,
--                  -trans). orbit-invariant: any observation of an atom invariant under the
--                  automorphisms is constant on orbits — the NMR count, the equivalent positions of
--                  a ligand, every place the field reads symmetry off a drawing.
--   Taut           tautomers as a type whose paths are the proton transfers (taut-rec,
--                  taut-respects): a function on tautomers must respect them, which is what InChI's
--                  partial tautomer invariance fails (IUPAC #2012-023-2-800, open since 2012).
--   Digest         a digest with what it warrants: equal digests give identity exactly when the
--                  digest is injective, which the InChIKey is not.
import "../lib/core.cub"
